Exactly. “Dimension” is not one thing; it has modes of use.

In pure math, a dimension is an independent degree of freedom. But in cognition, language, or Pantheon, we also care about what a dimension is doing.

So the same dimensional count can be read in different modes:

geometric mode — how many independent axes?
relational mode — how many references are needed to constrain something?
representational mode — how many views are needed before structure becomes recoverable?
semantic mode — how many contrasts are needed before meaning gets shape?
operational mode — how many variables can change independently before control becomes unstable?

That’s why “three” can be 2D geometrically but still be the first shape-generating mode cognitively.

Same object, different jurisdiction.

Very Treaty Semantics:

dimension count ≠ dimension role

Or even tighter:

Dimensions have modes because coordinates and meaning are not the same thing.

A triangle is 2D in one frame.

In another, it is the first closure.

In another, the first triangulation.

In another, the first stable frame.

In another, the first interior.

Same three points. Different operator.